/! What I Learned					
section
	.container
		.row
			.col-lg-12
				h2 What I learned
				p.lead.mb-3 Building CaptiveColour Cosmetics gave me firsthand experience with what it takes to turn an idea into a business and bring a physical product to market. I learned how to move from an initial concept through market research, brand development, product sourcing, testing, ecommerce, launch, and ongoing promotion while making decisions across many different parts of the business.
				p.lead Working directly with manufacturers and cosmetic labs taught me how to evaluate potential partners, communicate product requirements, compare formulas and packaging options, test samples, and refine products before committing to larger orders. It also showed me how interconnected product quality, packaging, branding, pricing, and customer expectations are.
				p.lead Customer experience became a major part of how I approached the brand. I had to think beyond individual touchpoints and consider the full journey, from discovering CaptiveColour through social media or an event, to exploring products online, placing an order, receiving the packaging, and ultimately using the product. Each interaction needed to feel consistent with the brand while making it easy for customers to understand the products and feel confident purchasing from a new cosmetics company.
				p.lead Launching and promoting CaptiveColour also expanded my experience in marketing and community building. I coordinated events, developed social and email content, worked with brand ambassadors, researched influencer opportunities, and networked to build awareness from the ground up. This taught me the importance of meeting customers where they are and creating multiple ways for them to discover and engage with a new brand.
				p.lead Most importantly, CaptiveColour taught me to look at a business as an interconnected experience rather than a collection of individual deliverables. I learned to balance customer needs with creative direction, business goals, budgets, timelines, vendor relationships, and marketing priorities. That broader perspective continues to influence how I approach UX, web design, and digital strategy today.
